---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import { authorSchema, authorName } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import PlanTheJob from "../components/PlanTheJob.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";

const moreInCategory = [
  {
    "title": "Best Mattress Brands",
    "url": "/best-mattress-brands/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Mattress for Back Pain",
    "url": "/best-mattress-for-back-pain/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Mattress for Side Sleepers",
    "url": "/best-mattress-for-side-sleepers/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Mattress Toppers",
    "url": "/best-mattress-topper/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Mattress Protectors",
    "url": "/best-mattress-protector/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Mattress for Heavy People",
    "url": "/best-mattress-for-heavy-people/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Mattress for Seniors",
    "url": "/best-mattress-for-seniors/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Mattress Prices and Deals",
    "url": "/mattress-prices-and-deals/"
  }
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-11-13", modified: "2026-11-13" });
const title = "7 Top Rated Mattresses in 2026: Foam, Hybrid and Budget Picks";
const description =
  "The top rated mattresses on Amazon for 2026: memory foam, hybrid, cooling, firm and budget picks, with firmness by sleep position, trials and warranties explained.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    "id": "nectar-classic-12",
    "name": "Nectar Classic 12\" Memory Foam Mattress",
    "category": "All-Foam, Medium Firm, 12 Inch",
    "badge": "Best Overall",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Medium-firm all-foam feel that suits back sleepers and many combination sleepers",
      "Nectar states a 365-night trial, one of the longest sold on Amazon",
      "Nectar's Forever Warranty is named in the listing title",
      "Cooling top layer and cover, per the maker",
      "Sold in twin through California king, plus adjustable-base bundles"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Memory foam sink is slower to move on than a hybrid",
      "Returns on Amazon orders can involve both Amazon and Nectar",
      "Heavier than budget foam beds, about 69 lb for a full per the listing"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The safest single recommendation: a medium-firm foam bed with a full year to decide and a lifetime warranty behind it.",
    "description": "The Nectar Classic is the mattress to start with if you want one bed that suits most people and gives you the most time to change your mind. It is a 12-inch all-foam mattress that Nectar rates medium firm, with five layers under a cooling cover: a contouring memory foam comfort layer, transition foam, and a thick base that keeps it from bottoming out. That firmness is the reason it ranks first. Medium firm is the setting most back sleepers and many combination sleepers land on, and it is firm enough that heavier partners do not sink to the base. The spec that separates it from cheaper foam beds is the policy, not the foam. Nectar states a 365-night home trial and a Forever Warranty, and both appear in the Amazon listing title, which matters for a purchase you cannot try in a store. The trade-off is the memory foam feel itself: you sink in slowly and it takes a moment to change position, which some combination sleepers dislike. Owners who sleep hot sometimes report it runs warmer than a hybrid. If you want more bounce and airflow, buy the Casper Original Hybrid below; if you are on a tight budget, the Zinus does the basic job for a fraction of the price. For a single bed that works for the most sleepers, this is it."
  },
  {
    "id": "casper-original-hybrid",
    "name": "Casper Original Hybrid Mattress",
    "category": "Hybrid, Zoned Foam Over Encased Coils, 11 Inch",
    "badge": "Best Hybrid",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Zoned support: softer foam under the shoulders, firmer under the hips, per Casper",
      "Encased coils give more bounce and airflow than all-foam beds",
      "Firmer perimeter for sitting on the edge",
      "Casper lists a 100-night trial and a 10-year limited warranty",
      "Sold by size on Amazon, twin through California king"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Costs much more than the budget hybrids on this list",
      "Some owners report the edge is only average for a hybrid",
      "Listings disagree on whether it is medium or medium firm"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The best hybrid here for couples and combination sleepers who want bounce, airflow and zoned support.",
    "description": "The Casper Original Hybrid is the pick for people who want the pressure relief of foam without the stuck-in feeling. It is an 11-inch hybrid: Casper puts its foam comfort layers over a core of individually encased coils, and that coil core is what changes the experience. You move across it easily, air moves through it, and it pushes back when you shift. The spec that matters is the zoned support. Casper makes the foam softer under the shoulders and firmer under the hips, waist and lower back, which is the combination side sleepers and back sleepers both need: the shoulder can sink while the heaviest part of the body stays level. Casper lists the feel as medium, a 100-night trial and a 10-year limited warranty, and the foams as CertiPUR-US certified. Encased coils also limit motion transfer, so a partner getting up is less likely to wake you than on an innerspring. The trade-offs are price, which sits well above the Linenspa and Ashley hybrids here, and an edge that owners describe as fine rather than exceptional. If you mainly sleep on your side and like a softer bed, look at our list of the best mattresses for side sleepers; if budget is the main constraint, the Ashley Chime is the closest cheaper hybrid."
  },
  {
    "id": "purple-mattress",
    "name": "Purple Mattress (GelFlex Grid)",
    "category": "2\" GelFlex Grid Over Foam, Medium Firm, 9.25 Inch",
    "badge": "Best Cooling",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "2-inch GelFlex Grid comfort layer that Purple says does not trap body heat",
      "Open grid moves air better than solid memory foam",
      "Responsive feel: you float rather than sink",
      "Purple states a 100-night trial and a 10-year limited warranty",
      "Sold in twin XL through split king on Amazon"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "The grid feel is unusual and some people dislike it",
      "Purple asks for at least 21 nights before a return",
      "Only 9.25 inches thick, so it can look thin on a tall frame"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The pick for hot sleepers who find memory foam stuffy and want pressure relief that stays cool.",
    "description": "The Purple Mattress is the one to buy if heat is the reason you are replacing your current bed. Instead of a memory foam comfort layer, it uses a 2-inch layer of Purple's GelFlex Grid, a stretchy polymer formed into open columns, over a foam base. Purple's case for it is simple: the columns collapse under pressure points like the shoulder and hip, stay firm elsewhere, and leave open channels for air, so the bed does not hold heat the way dense memory foam can. Purple rates the feel medium firm and the mattress stands 9.25 inches tall. The spec to understand before you buy is the feel, not the height. The grid feels like nothing else: springy, slightly squishy, with almost no sink. Many people love it within a week; some never get used to it, which is why the trial matters. Purple states a 100-night trial and a 10-year limited warranty, and asks owners to sleep on it for at least 21 nights before starting a return. It is also heavy for its size and the cover should stay on. If you want a more familiar feel with some of the same airflow, the Casper hybrid is the safer choice. If heat is your only complaint about a bed you otherwise like, a cooling mattress topper costs far less."
  },
  {
    "id": "serta-sleeptrue-firm-12",
    "name": "Serta Sleeptrue Firm 12\" Memory Foam Mattress",
    "category": "All-Foam, Firm, Gel-Swirled Memory Foam, 12 Inch",
    "badge": "Best Firm",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Firm feel for stomach sleepers and back sleepers who dislike sinking",
      "Cooling gel swirled into the memory foam, per Serta",
      "Serta lists a 100-night trial and a 10-year limited warranty",
      "Made in the USA with global components, per the listing",
      "CertiPUR-US certified foams"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Too firm for most side sleepers",
      "Serta says to allow up to 72 hours to expand and one to two weeks to air out",
      "All-foam, so it has less bounce than a hybrid"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A firm, well-backed foam bed from a household name, for people who want to lie on the mattress rather than in it.",
    "description": "The Serta Sleeptrue Firm is the pick for stomach sleepers and anyone who has woken up on a soft bed feeling like they spent the night in a hammock. It is a 12-inch all-foam mattress that Serta labels firm, with cooling gel swirled through the memory foam and a dense support base underneath. The spec that matters is the firmness rating, and it is unusual to find a true firm label on a mattress in a box: most brands aim for medium firm because it suits the most people. For stomach sleepers, firm is the point. A softer bed lets the hips sink below the shoulders, which arches the lower back; a firm surface keeps the body flatter. Many back sleepers who carry more weight also prefer a firmer foam bed. Serta lists a 100-night trial, a 10-year limited warranty, CertiPUR-US certified foams, and US manufacture with global components. Serta also says to give it up to 72 hours to expand fully and one to two weeks for any new-foam smell to clear, so plan the delivery around that. The trade-off is plain: most side sleepers will find it too hard at the shoulder and hip. If you sleep on your side, choose the Nectar or Casper instead, and if you want a firm bed with more bounce, a firm hybrid is worth the extra money."
  },
  {
    "id": "zinus-green-tea-12",
    "name": "Zinus 12 Inch Green Tea Memory Foam Mattress",
    "category": "All-Foam, Green Tea Infused, 12 Inch, Budget",
    "badge": "Best Budget Foam",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "One of the least expensive 12-inch foam mattresses on Amazon",
      "Green tea infused foams, CertiPUR-US certified per the listing",
      "Listed as fiberglass free",
      "Also sold in 6, 8 and 10 inch profiles for bunks and guest beds",
      "Light enough for one person to move, about 62 lb for a queen"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Owners report a noticeable new-foam smell at first",
      "Likely to soften sooner than the premium foam beds above",
      "Zinus labels it medium in some listings and medium firm in others"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The budget foam bed to buy for a guest room, a first apartment or a child's room.",
    "description": "The Zinus Green Tea Memory Foam Mattress is the budget pick because it does the basics well for very little money. It is a 12-inch all-foam bed with green tea infused memory foam over comfort and base foam, shipped compressed in a box and listed as CertiPUR-US certified and fiberglass free. Zinus describes the feel as medium in some listings and medium firm in others; owners generally describe it as softer than the Nectar. The spec that matters at this price is thickness. The 12-inch profile has enough foam above the base that most adults under about 230 pounds do not feel the bottom, which is the main failure of the thinner 6 and 8 inch versions. Those thinner models are better used for bunks, trundles and kids. The trade-off is longevity and smell. Budget foams use lower-density foam than premium beds, and that is where softening and body impressions start, so expect fewer years from it than from the Nectar or Serta. Some buyers also report a strong smell at unboxing that fades over several days with the windows open. Buy it for a guest room, a first apartment or a stopgap bed. If it will be your main bed for the next decade, or if you weigh more than average, read our guide to the best mattresses for heavy people first."
  },
  {
    "id": "linenspa-10-hybrid",
    "name": "Linenspa 10 Inch Memory Foam and Spring Hybrid Mattress",
    "category": "Hybrid, Memory Foam Over Steel Coils, 10 Inch, Budget",
    "badge": "Best Budget Hybrid",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Coil support at a foam-mattress price",
      "Universal medium feel, per Linenspa, for guest rooms with mixed sleepers",
      "Linenspa lists a 10-year limited warranty",
      "Queen rated for 600 lb total, per the listing",
      "Also sold in short queen for RVs"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Continuous coil core transfers more motion than pocketed coils",
      "Cover is not removable or washable; spot clean only",
      "Thin comfort layers, so it may feel firm to lighter side sleepers"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The cheapest way to get coil support and bounce, best for guest rooms, RVs and kids.",
    "description": "The Linenspa 10 Inch Hybrid is the cheapest way on this list to get a coil core under you. Linenspa builds it in three parts: a 1.5-inch quilted memory foam cover, a 2.5-inch foam comfort layer, and a 6-inch base of tempered steel springs and support foam. The result is a mattress with the bounce and edge feel of an innerspring and a little memory foam softness on top. Linenspa calls the feel a universal medium and pitches it for guest rooms, which is exactly where it fits best: a bed that has to suit whoever sleeps on it. The spec that matters is the coil type. The listing describes a continuous coil, which is supportive and cheap but links the springs together, so movement on one side travels further across the bed than it does on a pocketed-coil hybrid like the Casper. A light sleeper sharing with a restless partner will notice. Linenspa lists a 10-year limited warranty and a 600-pound total weight limit on the queen, and the foams are CertiPUR-US certified. The cover does not come off, so a mattress protector is close to mandatory. The thin comfort layers can feel firm at the shoulder for lighter side sleepers. For a primary bed for two, spend more on the Ashley Chime or Casper hybrid."
  },
  {
    "id": "ashley-chime-12-hybrid",
    "name": "Signature Design by Ashley Chime 12 Inch Hybrid Mattress",
    "category": "Hybrid, Gel Memory Foam Over Pocketed Coils, 12 Inch",
    "badge": "Best Value Hybrid",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Pocketed coils at a price well below the premium hybrids",
      "Gel memory foam and quilted foam comfort layers, per Ashley",
      "Medium firm label suits back and combination sleepers",
      "Listed as fiberglass free",
      "Sold in full through California king, plus softer plush versions"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Listings disagree on coil count and some list a plush version under the same name",
      "Thinner comfort layers than the Casper",
      "Edge support is modest for a hybrid, owners commonly report"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The value hybrid: pocketed coils and a medium-firm feel for a budget price, if you check you have the medium firm version.",
    "description": "The Ashley Chime 12 Inch Hybrid is the middle ground between the Linenspa and the Casper: a pocketed-coil hybrid at a budget price. Ashley lists the comfort stack as 1.5 inches of soft quilt foam, 1 inch of support foam and 0.75 inch of gel memory foam over individually wrapped coils, and labels the main version medium firm. The spec that matters is those pocketed coils. Because each spring moves on its own, the Chime isolates a partner's movement better than a continuous-coil bed and supports the body in more places, while keeping the airflow and bounce that make a hybrid easier to move on than all-foam. That is a real upgrade over the Linenspa for couples. The trade-off is a thinner comfort layer than the premium hybrids, so heavier side sleepers may feel the coils at the shoulder, and owners commonly report that the edge compresses when you sit on it. Check the listing carefully before you order: Ashley sells medium firm, plush and ultra plush versions under the Chime name, and listings disagree on coil count, so confirm the firmness in the title. The mattress is listed as fiberglass free. Buy it if you want a hybrid for a main bedroom on a budget; if you can stretch, the Casper adds zoned support, a firmer edge and a stated trial."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Top Rated Mattresses",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-mattress/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best mattress?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most people, the best mattress is a medium-firm bed with a long trial, and the Nectar Classic 12-inch memory foam mattress fits that best on Amazon, with a 365-night trial and lifetime warranty stated by Nectar. Couples and hot sleepers may prefer the Casper Original Hybrid or the Purple Mattress, and budget shoppers the Zinus Green Tea."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best rated mattress on Amazon?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The best rated mattress on Amazon depends on what you need, since ratings mix guest-room beds with primary beds. Among heavily reviewed models, the Nectar Classic is the strongest all-round foam bed, the Casper Original Hybrid the strongest hybrid, and the Zinus Green Tea and Linenspa hybrid the most popular budget beds. Read reviews from people with your sleep position and weight."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a mattress in a box as good as a store mattress?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Often, yes. Many brands now sell the same construction in a box that they sell in stores, and a boxed bed usually costs less because it ships compressed. The difference is that you cannot try it first, so the trial period and return terms matter more. Allow up to 72 hours for it to expand fully before judging it."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What firmness is best for a good mattress?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Medium firm suits the largest number of people, especially back sleepers and combination sleepers. Side sleepers usually prefer medium or medium soft so the shoulder and hip can sink, and stomach sleepers usually prefer firm so the hips do not dip. Lighter people feel beds as firmer, heavier people as softer, so adjust the label to your weight."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a hybrid or memory foam mattress better?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Neither is better for everyone. Memory foam contours closely and absorbs a partner's movement but can sleep warmer. A hybrid adds coils for bounce, airflow and edge support and is easier to move on. Choose foam for motion isolation, a hybrid if you run hot, change positions often or weigh more than average."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long does a mattress in a box last?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most last about seven to ten years, depending on foam density, coil type and the weight they carry. Budget all-foam beds tend to soften sooner; pocketed-coil hybrids and denser foams usually last longer. Rotating the bed every few months, using a proper base and keeping a protector on all help it reach the longer end of that range."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long does a new mattress smell last?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The new-foam smell is strongest at unboxing and usually fades within a few days to two weeks. Serta, for example, advises one to two weeks to air out. Open windows, run a fan and leave the bed uncovered for the first day to speed it up. CertiPUR-US certified foams are tested for certain emissions."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: authorName(Astro.url.pathname),
    section: "Bedroom & Sleep",
    tags: [
      "top rated mattresses",
      "best mattresses",
      "best rated mattress",
      "best mattress in a box",
      "good mattress",
      "mattress buying guide"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #38BDF8 0, transparent 45%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 60%, #FF4500 0, transparent 40%);"></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated November 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Top Rated <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Mattresses</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          Firmness for the way you sleep, a build that suits your body heat, and a trial that lets you change your mind. Seven mattresses on Amazon that get all three right.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#nectar-classic-12"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#top-picks"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            See the Rankings
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="12.4k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#what-matters" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. What Makes a Mattress Top Rated</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. The 7 Top Rated Mattresses</a>
          <a href="#firmness" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Firmness by Sleep Position</a>
          <a href="#types" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Memory Foam vs Hybrid vs Innerspring</a>
          <a href="#setup" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Mattress in a Box Setup</a>
          <a href="#trials" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Trials and Warranties</a>
          <a href="#lifespan" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. How Long a Mattress Lasts</a>
          <a href="#foundation" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Foundations and Frames</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A good mattress is not the one with the most foam layers. It is the one whose firmness matches how you sleep, whose build matches how warm you run, and whose trial gives you time to find out.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="what-matters" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What Makes a Mattress Top Rated: Firmness, Build and the Fine Print
          </h2>

          <p>
            The top rated mattresses on Amazon are not the ones with the most layers or the longest list of foam names. They are the ones that match three things to the person sleeping on them: a firmness that suits the way you sleep, a construction that suits how warm you run and whether you share the bed, and a trial and warranty that protect you if the first two guesses are wrong.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Firmness Comes First</h3>

          <p>
            Firmness decides whether your shoulders and hips sink the right amount. Side sleepers need more give, stomach sleepers need less, and back sleepers sit in the middle. Most mattresses in a box are sold as medium firm because it suits the largest group, and that is why medium firm beds dominate best-seller lists. The firmness section below explains how to choose by sleep position.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Construction Decides Feel and Heat</h3>

          <p>
            All-foam beds hug the body and absorb motion; hybrids put foam over coils for bounce and airflow. Neither is better in general. A couple with a restless partner may prefer foam, and a hot sleeper may prefer a hybrid. We explain the trade-offs in the memory foam vs hybrid vs innerspring section.
          </p>

