        <div id="firmness" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How to Choose Firmness by Sleep Position
          </h2>

          <p>
            Firmness is a feel, not a measurement, and every brand uses its own scale. Still, the logic is consistent: the goal is a mattress that lets the widest parts of your body sink just enough to keep the spine in a straight, relaxed line. Your main sleep position decides how much sink that is.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Side Sleepers: Medium to Medium Soft</h3>

          <p>
            On your side, the shoulder and hip carry most of your weight. A firm bed pushes back on both and can leave you with numb arms and sore hips by morning, while a medium or medium-soft bed lets them sink in. Look for a thick comfort layer of memory foam or a zoned hybrid like the Casper. Our <a href="/best-mattress-for-side-sleepers/">best mattress for side sleepers</a> guide covers this position in detail.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Back Sleepers: Medium Firm</h3>

          <p>
            On your back, weight is spread more evenly, and the lower back needs support so it does not dip. Medium firm suits most back sleepers, which is why the Nectar ranks first. Many people with lower back discomfort prefer a medium-firm bed; our <a href="/best-mattress-for-back-pain/">best mattress for back pain</a> guide goes further. A mattress is not a treatment, so talk to your doctor or physical therapist about persistent pain.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Stomach Sleepers: Firm</h3>

          <p>
            On your stomach, the hips are the heaviest point and they sink first. On a soft bed that arches the lower back. A firm mattress such as the Serta Sleeptrue Firm keeps the body flatter. Thin comfort layers are a feature here, not a flaw.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Combination Sleepers and Body Weight</h3>

          <p>
            If you change position through the night, choose medium firm and favour a hybrid, which is easier to turn on. Body weight shifts every recommendation: people under about 130 pounds tend to feel a bed as firmer than the label, and people over about 230 pounds feel it as softer and need thicker, denser support. If that is you, see our guide to the <a href="/best-mattress-for-heavy-people/">best mattresses for heavy people</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Memory Foam vs Hybrid vs Innerspring: Which Type to Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            Almost every mattress sold online is one of three builds. The type sets the feel, the temperature, the bounce and, to a large extent, how long the bed lasts, so pick the type before you compare brands.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Memory Foam</h3>

          <p>
            All-foam mattresses use layers of memory foam and polyfoam over a dense base. They contour closely, absorb motion well, and are quiet, which makes them good for couples where one partner is restless. The downsides are heat, because solid foam holds warmth, and a slow, sinking feel that some combination sleepers find hard to move on. Brands add gel, graphite or open-cell foam to reduce heat. The Nectar, Serta and Zinus on our list are all-foam.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Hybrid</h3>

          <p>
            A hybrid puts several inches of foam on top of a coil core. The coils add bounce, airflow and a firmer edge, and pocketed coils, where each spring is wrapped individually, keep motion from travelling across the bed. Hybrids usually cost more and weigh more than foam beds. The Casper, Ashley Chime and Linenspa are hybrids, and the coil type is the main difference between them.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Innerspring</h3>

          <p>
            A traditional innerspring has a coil core and only a thin padded top. It is bouncy, cool and inexpensive, but it gives little pressure relief and transfers motion. Pure innersprings are now rare in boxes; most budget beds sold as innersprings are really thin-layer hybrids.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How to Choose Between Them</h3>

          <p>
            Choose foam if you share the bed with a restless sleeper and do not run hot. Choose a hybrid if you run hot, change position often, sit on the edge of the bed, or weigh more than average. Choose a grid or latex bed like the Purple if you want pressure relief without sink. For a closer look at what each brand does best, see our <a href="/best-mattress-brands/">best mattress brands</a> guide.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="setup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Mattress in a Box: Setup, Expansion and Off-Gassing
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every pick on this list ships compressed and rolled in a box. That is what makes the best mattress in a box cheaper to ship and easier to carry upstairs, but it adds a few steps a showroom bed does not have.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Unbox on the Bed Frame</h3>

          <p>
            Carry the box to the bedroom before you open it; a queen can weigh 60 to 90 pounds and is far easier to move while rolled. Place the roll on the foundation, cut the outer plastic carefully with the cutter many brands include, and unroll it before cutting the inner wrap. Never use a knife against the mattress itself.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Give It Time to Expand</h3>

          <p>
            Most foams recover their shape within a few hours, but makers commonly ask for 24 to 72 hours before full height and feel. Serta and Linenspa both state a window of up to 72 hours. Do not judge the firmness on night one; it often softens slightly over the first weeks as the foam breaks in.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Air Out the New-Foam Smell</h3>

          <p>
            New polyurethane foam releases a smell, usually called off-gassing, that is strongest at unboxing and fades over days. Serta says one to two weeks. Open the windows, run a fan, and leave the sheets off for the first day if you can. CertiPUR-US certification, which every foam pick here lists, means the foam is tested for certain emissions and substances. If you are sensitive to smell, unbox it in a spare room first.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 4: Protect It Before the First Night</h3>

          <p>
            Put a <a href="/best-mattress-protector/">mattress protector</a> on before you sleep on it. A stain can void some warranties, and most covers on boxed beds are not removable or washable.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="trials" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Trial Periods and Warranties: Read the Terms, Not the Headline
          </h2>

          <p>
            The trial and warranty on a mattress are worth as much as the foam, because they decide what happens when a bed does not work out. They also vary far more than the marketing suggests.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Sleep Trials</h3>

          <p>
            Makers state trials of 100 nights (Casper, Purple, Serta) up to 365 nights (Nectar). Most require you to keep the bed for a minimum period before a return; Purple, for example, asks for at least 21 nights. Your body needs a few weeks to adjust to a new surface, so the minimum is reasonable. Some budget brands state no trial at all, in which case the standard Amazon return window applies, and returning a bed that no longer fits in its box is a hassle.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Buying on Amazon vs Direct</h3>

          <p>
            A trial stated by the maker usually applies to an authorized seller on Amazon, but the return process can involve both the brand and Amazon, and some owners report being passed between the two. Before you order, check that the seller is the brand or Amazon itself and read the return terms on that listing. Our <a href="/mattress-prices-and-deals/">mattress prices and deals</a> guide covers when to buy.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Warranties</h3>

          <p>
            Ten-year limited warranties are standard, and Nectar states a lifetime warranty. Read what is covered. Warranties usually cover visible sagging or body impressions past a stated depth, often around 1 to 1.5 inches, and manufacturing defects. They typically exclude normal softening, stains and use on an unsupportive base. Keep the law tag on and keep your receipt.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="lifespan" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How Long Does a Mattress Last? Usually 7 to 10 Years
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most guidance puts the useful life of a mattress at roughly seven to ten years. The real number depends on the type, the density of the foam, the weight it carries and how it is supported.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">By Type</h3>

          <p>
            Budget all-foam beds tend to soften first, because lower-density foam loses its shape sooner. Denser premium foam and hybrids with pocketed coils usually hold up longer, and latex lasts longest of all. A mattress in a guest room that sees a few nights a month will last far longer than the bed you sleep on every night.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Signs It Is Time to Replace It</h3>

          <p>
            Look for a visible dip where you sleep, sagging at the edge, waking stiff and loosening up as the day goes on, sleeping better in hotels than at home, or springs you can feel. If the dip is within the warranty's stated depth and the bed is young, file a warranty claim before you buy a new one.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How to Make It Last Longer</h3>

          <p>
            Rotate the mattress head to foot every three to six months, use the foundation the maker specifies, keep a protector on, and avoid regularly sitting in one spot on the edge. A <a href="/best-mattress-topper/">mattress topper</a> can buy an older but still supportive bed a year or two, though it will not fix a sag.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="foundation" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. What to Put Under It: Foundations, Frames and Bed Height
          </h2>

          <p>
            A good mattress on the wrong base will feel worse and wear faster, and many warranties require proper support.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Slats, Platforms and Box Springs</h3>

          <p>
            Most foam and hybrid beds in a box are made for a solid platform or slats spaced no more than about three inches apart; check the maker's number. Old box springs with worn springs or wide gaps can let foam sag between them. Most foam and hybrid beds also work on adjustable bases, which is why brands sell bundles.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Bed Height</h3>

          <p>
            A 12-inch mattress on a high frame can make getting in and out awkward, which matters for older sleepers. Our <a href="/best-mattress-for-seniors/">best mattress for seniors</a> guide covers edge support and height.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the Firmness Label Instead of the Firmness You Need</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A medium firm bed from one brand can feel firm from another. Choose by your sleep position and weight, then use the trial to confirm it, rather than trusting the number on the box.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Judging the Mattress on the First Night</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Foam needs up to 72 hours to expand and weeks to break in, and your body needs time to adjust. Most trials require a minimum stay for that reason. Give it three to four weeks.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring Who Is Selling It</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The maker's trial and warranty usually depend on buying from an authorized seller. On Amazon, confirm the seller is the brand or Amazon before ordering, and keep the receipt.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting a New Mattress on an Old Base</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A sagging box spring or widely spaced slats can undo a new mattress in months and void the warranty. Check the maker's support requirements.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Protector</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most boxed mattress covers do not come off, and stains can void a warranty. A thin waterproof protector costs little and protects both.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

